diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan-validator.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan-validator.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5fabfdf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan-validator.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,208 @@
+{#
+  Cisco SD-WAN vBond (validator) day-0 bootstrap (KNE startup-config
+  config-drive, pre-SSH).
+
+  A vBond is the vEdge SOFTWARE with the validator PERSONALITY, and the
+  personality comes from CONFIG, not the image: `system vbond <address> local`
+  is what makes this box a vBond at all. Without it the device reports
+  Personality: vedge and vManage refuses to enroll it as a validator (and the
+  old vSmart-image shim reported Personality: vsmart with the same refusal --
+  SYSTD0001, measured live 2026-08-31).
+
+  Scope discipline, same as the controller template: management + identity
+  only. Certificates, serial lists and overlay onboarding belong to vManage
+  (see the header of cisco_sdwan-controller.j2 for the three-round failure
+  that taught this). The `vbond ... local` stanza is IDENTITY, not overlay --
+  the box is not a validator without it.
+
+  eth0 STAYS IN VPN 512. The device will not allow anything else, and it says
+  so plainly -- moving it aborts the whole day-0:
+
+    Aborted: 'vpn': Cannot delete eth0 interface from management vpn (512)
+
+  (demo-lab run 74108, 2026-08-31.) On vEdge software -- which is what a vBond
+  runs, the console prompt says `vedge(...)` -- eth0 is THE management
+  interface and VPN 512 is THE management VPN. It is not a placement choice.
+
+  That refusal also settles a wrong hypothesis worth recording, because the
+  evidence for it looked strong. vsmart and vmanage answer tcp/22 with eth0 in
+  VPN 0 while vbond did not, so the VPN looked causal. It is not: those two run
+  different IMAGES whose factory config puts eth0 in VPN 0 to begin with. The
+  correlation was real and the conclusion was still wrong.
+
+  So this declares the interface where the factory already has it, which is an
+  update rather than a duplicate (`vpn 0 interface eth0` is what produced
+  "values are not unique: eth0" in run 73997) and rather than a move (which is
+  refused outright). Whether sshd then answers is a SEPARATE question from
+  where the interface lives -- do not conflate them again.
+#}
+{#
+  FABRIC IDENTITY. These four are what make a Viptela box a member of an
+  overlay rather than a lone appliance: without system-ip, site-id,
+  organization-name and a vbond pointer it has nothing to dial and nothing to
+  identify itself with, and no control connection can form.
+
+  The sdwan module derives all four (_push_overlay_facts) because they are
+  properties of the FABRIC, not of one node -- a template renders with node
+  scope and cannot look up the validator's address or keep an organization
+  byte-identical across devices.
+
+  Measured 2026-08-31 on demo-lab: the module computed them for every node and
+  no template emitted any of them. `show running-config system | include
+  vbond` on the live vSmart returned NOTHING -- it knew its own hostname and
+  nothing about the fabric it was supposed to control.
+#}
+system
+ host-name {{ inventory_hostname }}
+ admin-tech-on-failure
+{#
+  FABRIC IDENTITY ONLY WHEN THE NODE RUNS THE sdwan MODULE. Without the module
+  the device still boots (host-name, admin, VPN 512) and simply belongs to no
+  overlay -- that is the contract the KNE provider documents for a Viptela node
+  outside a fabric. WITH the module every value below is required and the
+  strict renderer fails on a missing one; the module itself reports a missing
+  validator by name before it gets that far. No per-attribute default: a
+  guessed system-ip or vbond is the placeholder fabric this file once shipped.
+#}
+{% if sdwan is defined %}
+ system-ip               {{ sdwan.system_ip }}
+ site-id                 {{ sdwan.site_id }}
+ organization-name       "{{ sdwan.organization }}"
+ vbond                   {{ sdwan.vbond_address }}{{ " local vbond-only" if sdwan.is_validator | default(false) else "" }}
+{% endif %}
+!
+{#
+  THE DECLARED PASSWORD, not the factory one.
+
+  This used to render `password admin`, and the factory->declared transition
+  happened later over vManage's REST API. That could not work in general: while
+  the box holds the factory password every /dataservice/* answers 302 ->
+  passwordReset.html on an otherwise valid session, so the API lane had to
+  perform the very transition that unblocked its own session. vManage also
+  refuses to KEEP the factory value ("USER0002: Password for admin user cannot
+  be configured to default/last password", measured live).
+
+  Day-0 is the right place: the image already FORCES a password change on first
+  boot, and the serial injector is already answering that prompt. Having it
+  answer with the declared value makes the transition a non-event rather than a
+  special case, and this stanza keeps the committed config agreeing with it.
+
+  sdwan.declared_password is resolved fabric-wide by the sdwan module, so this
+  file, the injector's answer and the API lane's login all read ONE value.
+#}
+aaa
+ user admin
+  password {{ sdwan.declared_password | default('admin') }}
+ !
+!
+{#
+  eth0 (KNE management) stays in VPN 512.
+
+  It was briefly moved to VPN 0 on the theory that the vBond's empty
+  management address was a DHCP problem. That was wrong and it broke the box:
+  vEdge software has no eth0 data port, so configuring one appears to abort
+  the day-0 commit, and the vBond came up with NO transport address at all --
+  "No route to host" from vManage, where it had previously pinged fine.
+
+  The real reason management cannot carry onboarding is unrelated to DHCP:
+  VPN 512 is a separate NETWORK NAMESPACE (ip netns: 512 / default / 65531),
+  and sshd listens on 0.0.0.0:22 and :830 in the DEFAULT namespace only. The
+  management address can never reach it. Onboarding therefore runs over the
+  transport address, which lives in the default namespace alongside sshd --
+  measured directly: a banner grab from vManage to the vSmart's transport
+  address returns SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_8.2 on both 22 and 830.
+#}
+vpn 512
+ interface eth0
+  ip dhcp-client
+  no shutdown
+ !
+!
+!
+{#
+  MANAGEMENT BOOTSTRAP (VPN 512). vManage onboards a controller by opening an
+  SSH/NETCONF session to it -- "Failed to get SSH session" is the API's own
+  words when it cannot -- and Viptela serves those in the MANAGEMENT VPN.
+  Measured: tcp/22 and tcp/830 stayed closed on a VPN 0 transport interface
+  both with a tunnel-interface (allow-service filters until the tunnel is up,
+  which needs the very certificates onboarding obtains) and without one
+  (nothing listens there). The KNE management port cannot carry it either --
+  every controller boots with eth0 Up/Up and NO address.
+#}
+{% for m in sdwan.mgmt_interfaces | default([]) %}
+vpn 512
+ interface ge0/{{ loop.index }}
+  ip address {{ m.ipv4 }}
+  no shutdown
+ !
+!
+{% endfor %}
+{#
+  TRANSPORT BOOTSTRAP -- an ADDRESS, deliberately without a tunnel-interface.
+
+  Measured across four rig runs. Onboarding needs vManage to reach the device
+  over NETCONF, because the DEVICE has to generate its own CSR against its own
+  private key; vManage signing one for it produces "CSR not generated /
+  Certificate doesn't match CSR" on install.
+
+  Neither obvious path carried that session:
+
+    transport WITH tunnel-interface  RETRACTED 2026-09-02. This said the
+                                     allow-service filter does not pass until
+                                     the tunnel is up (RST-ACK on tcp/22 and
+                                     tcp/830). The validator itself disproves
+                                     it: it has carried this exact stanza in
+                                     every run, and from vManage's own vshell
+                                     its tcp/830 answers SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_8.2.
+                                     A tunnel-interface does NOT close
+                                     onboarding, and without one the interface
+                                     is not a WAN interface at all.
+
+    management (eth0)                unusable here: ALL THREE controllers show
+                                     eth0 Up/Up with NO ipv4 address. The
+                                     KubeVirt pod address answers from the
+                                     cluster only through masquerade NAT, so
+                                     no such path exists from inside the lab.
+                                     Confirmed from vManage itself: every
+                                     management probe returned no-answer.
+
+  A plain addressed interface has no filter, so netconf answers and enrollment
+  can proceed. Tunnels, colours and policy are vManage's to push afterwards --
+  which is the real division of labour anyway.
+#}
+{% for t in sdwan.transport_interfaces | default([]) %}
+vpn 0
+ interface ge0/{{ loop.index0 }}
+  ip address {{ t.ipv4 }}
+  {#
+    The VALIDATOR alone needs the tunnel-interface + allow-service block, and
+    this is measured rather than inherited from the sibling templates.
+
+    vEdge software default-denies on VPN 0; the vSmart/vManage controller
+    images do not. Same rig, same subnet, same moment:
+
+      vManage -> vsmart 172.16.0.2:22   SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_8.2
+      vManage -> vbond  172.16.0.3:22   hangs (ICMP 2/2, 0% loss)
+
+    A hang rather than a refusal is the tell: the vBond IS listening --
+    `ss -tln` on the box shows 0.0.0.0:22 and 0.0.0.0:830 -- so the packets
+    are being dropped in front of it, not rejected by a closed port.
+
+    An earlier revision carried these same lines and appeared not to help,
+    but that reading came from vManage's /device/tools/nping, which reports
+    "closed" for ports a direct connection answers on. Two revisions were
+    built on believing that tool; this block is restored on the evidence of
+    a banner grab instead.
+  #}
+  tunnel-interface
+   encapsulation ipsec
+   allow-service all
+   allow-service netconf
+   allow-service sshd
+  !
+  no shutdown
+ !
+!
+{% endfor %}
+
+{% include 'cisco_sdwan.rootca.j2' %}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.cloudconfig.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.cloudconfig.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..11c0df8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.cloudconfig.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,349 @@
+{#
+  Cisco SD-WAN controller day-0 as a cloud-init document, for the KNE provider.
+
+  WHY A SEED AND NOT A CONSOLE. The appliance's first-boot wizard (forced initial
+  password, and on vManage the persona and storage menus) is a stateful console
+  dialog. Driving it from an expect script produced a whole family of failures
+  (blind newlines read as empty passwords, a parked "Re-enter password:",
+  "System Initializing" counted as a refusal). Seeding the ConfD init file makes
+  the wizard never fire: the admin user is CONFIGURED, not "still the default",
+  and -- measured on 20.7.1 -- that is also the difference between the vManage
+  API refusing admin/admin and accepting it. This is how containerlab/vrnetlab
+  boot the same images.
+
+  WHAT WAS MEASURED (2026-09-02, cisco_sdwan-controller 20.7.1, controls each):
+    * cloud-init runs fully on these images; write_files lands; the config-drive
+      KNE already attaches is consumed. The earlier "never consumed" finding was
+      on a vEdge image, which is why vEdge/cEdge are NOT on this path.
+    * the password MUST be a crypt hash. A plaintext <password> in a full init
+      file (with vpn stanzas) was stored verbatim and every login was refused;
+      the SAME value as a $6$ hash logged straight in.
+    * element order: personality, device-model, host-name, system-ip, site-id,
+      organization-name, sp-organization-name, vbond, aaa. With that order
+      host-name takes; out of order it silently did not.
+    * /usr/share/viptela/root-ca.crt written by cloud-init is INSTALLED as the
+      root chain at first boot (show certificate root-ca-cert lists it), so the
+      enterprise root CA needs no exec and no console at all. The stock
+      symantec-root-ca.crt is blanked the way Cisco's own day-0 does.
+
+    * vSmart's INIT-PATH validator allows AT MOST ONE INTERFACE PER VPN
+      (bisected 2026-09-02 on six standalone boots, verdict at T+35 s each):
+        CDB boot error: Init transaction failed to validate:
+          /viptela-vpn:vpn/vpn-instance{0} : Maximum one tunnel interface allowed on vsmart
+      fires for two interfaces in VPN 0, for two in VPN 512 (the message then
+      names vpn-instance{512}), and with an explicit <tunnel-interface> on one
+      of them; one interface per VPN boots clean. The same two interfaces
+      "Commit complete." from the CLI at runtime. vManage and the validator
+      have no such rule. So the CONTROLLER seed carries eth0 (VPN 0: KNE
+      management, and the only namespace where sshd listens) and the FIRST
+      management interface (VPN 512), and the rest -- every transport
+      interface, any further management interface -- is rendered here as a
+      CLI overlay the day-0 injector pastes and commits after login (no wizard
+      to fight: the seed already configured admin).
+
+  Everything below comes from the same facts the CLI templates use; the CLI
+  templates remain the day-0 for clab/libvirt.
+#}
+{%- set OVERLAY_PATH = '/home/admin/skyforge-day0-overlay.cli' -%}
+{%- macro pw_hash() -%}{{ sdwan.declared_password_hash | default(sdwan.declared_password | default('admin')) }}{%- endmacro -%}
+{%- macro zcloud(personality, device_model, vbond_local, transport_if, mgmt_if, tunnel, seed_transport=true, seed_mgmt_max=0) -%}
+<config xmlns="http://tail-f.com/ns/config/1.0">
+  <system xmlns="http://viptela.com/system">
+    <personality>{{ personality }}</personality>
+{% if device_model %}
+    <device-model>{{ device_model }}</device-model>
+{% endif %}
+    <host-name>{{ inventory_hostname }}</host-name>
+{#
+  FABRIC IDENTITY ONLY WHEN THE NODE RUNS THE sdwan MODULE -- same rule as the
+  CLI templates. Outside a fabric the seed still configures the admin user and
+  the management VPN, so the wizard never fires and the node is reachable; it
+  just belongs to no overlay. With the module, every element is required and
+  the strict renderer refuses a missing one.
+#}
+{% if sdwan is defined %}
+    <system-ip>{{ sdwan.system_ip }}</system-ip>
+    <site-id>{{ sdwan.site_id }}</site-id>
+    <organization-name>{{ sdwan.organization }}</organization-name>
+    <sp-organization-name>{{ sdwan.organization }}</sp-organization-name>
+    <vbond>
+      <remote>{{ sdwan.vbond_address }}</remote>
+      <port>12346</port>
+{% if vbond_local %}
+      <local/>
+{% endif %}
+    </vbond>
+{% endif %}
+    <aaa>
+      <user>
+        <name>admin</name>
+        <password>{{ pw_hash() }}</password>
+        <group>netadmin</group>
+      </user>
+    </aaa>
+  </system>
+  <vpn xmlns="http://viptela.com/vpn">
+    <vpn-instance>
+      <vpn-id>0</vpn-id>
+{% if not vbond_local %}
+      <interface>
+        <if-name>eth0</if-name>
+        <ip><dhcp-client>true</dhcp-client></ip>
+        <shutdown>false</shutdown>
+      </interface>
+{% endif %}
+{% for t in sdwan.transport_interfaces | default([]) %}
+{% if seed_transport %}
+      <interface>
+        <if-name>{{ transport_if(loop) }}</if-name>
+        <ip><address>{{ t.ipv4 }}</address></ip>
+{% if tunnel %}
+        <tunnel-interface>
+{# ENCAPSULATION IS vEDGE-FAMILY ONLY. A vSmart/vManage tunnel-interface
+   carries control connections, not an IPsec data plane, and rejects the
+   attribute outright -- measured 2026-09-02 on a live 20.7.1 vSmart:
+     vsmart(config-interface-eth1)#   tunnel-interface
+     vsmart(config-tunnel-interface)#    encapsulation ipsec
+     ------------------------------------^
+     syntax error: unknown command
+   The validator is a vEdge and does take it, which is why its stanza has
+   carried encapsulation correctly all along. #}
+{% if personality == 'vedge' %}
+          <encapsulation><encap>ipsec</encap></encapsulation>
+{% endif %}
+          <color><value>{{ t.color | default("default") }}</value></color>
+          <allow-service>
+            <all>true</all>
+            <netconf>true</netconf>
+            <sshd>true</sshd>
+          </allow-service>
+        </tunnel-interface>
+{% endif %}
+        <shutdown>false</shutdown>
+      </interface>
+{% endif %}
+{% endfor %}
+    </vpn-instance>
+    <vpn-instance>
+      <vpn-id>512</vpn-id>
+{% if vbond_local %}
+      <interface>
+        <if-name>eth0</if-name>
+        <ip><dhcp-client>true</dhcp-client></ip>
+        <shutdown>false</shutdown>
+      </interface>
+{% endif %}
+{#
+  NO ROUTES IN THE SEED. An <ip><route> block here is accepted by ConfD's init
+  path and then silently DOES NOTHING -- measured on demo-lab (run 87451): the
+  vBond booted from a seed carrying three routes and answered
+  `show running-config vpn 0 ip route` with "% No entries found.", VPN 0
+  holding only its connected prefixes, while the vSmart -- which gets the same
+  routes through the CLI overlay -- had all three installed and could ping the
+  edge. Routes ride the overlay for every personality; see overlay_routes().
+#}
+{% for m in sdwan.mgmt_interfaces | default([]) %}
+{% if seed_mgmt_max == 0 or loop.index <= seed_mgmt_max %}
+      <interface>
+        <if-name>{{ mgmt_if(loop) }}</if-name>
+        <ip><address>{{ m.ipv4 }}</address></ip>
+        <shutdown>false</shutdown>
+      </interface>
+{% endif %}
+{% endfor %}
+    </vpn-instance>
+  </vpn>
+</config>
+{%- endmacro -%}
+{%- macro overlay_cli(transport_if, mgmt_if, skip_mgmt, encap=false, route=false) -%}
+{% if route and sdwan.vpn0_routes is defined and sdwan.vpn0_routes %}
+vpn 0
+{% for r in sdwan.vpn0_routes %}
+ ip route {{ r.prefix }} {{ r.nexthop }}
+{% endfor %}
+!
+{% endif %}
+{% for t in sdwan.transport_interfaces | default([]) %}
+{#
+  THE TUNNEL-INTERFACE IS WHAT MAKES THIS A WAN INTERFACE.
+
+  Without it Viptela counts the interface but not as transport:
+  "show control local-properties" reports number-active-wan-interfaces 0 and
+  number-vbond-peers 0, no control connection can form, and vManage leaves the
+  controller at deviceState BRING_UP with no host-name -- so its certificate
+  never reaches Installed no matter how good the CA is. Measured on sdwan-min
+  2026-09-02: vmanage and vsmart (no tunnel-interface) both sat at 0 WAN
+  interfaces with 0 control connections four hours after onboarding.
+
+  This is the CLI half. vSmart's init-path validator refuses two interfaces in
+  one VPN ("Maximum one tunnel interface allowed on vsmart"), so its transport
+  cannot ride the seed at all -- but the same two interfaces commit cleanly
+  from the CLI at runtime, which is why the overlay exists.
+
+  allow-service does NOT lock onboarding out, contrary to an earlier note in
+  this series: the VALIDATOR has carried this exact stanza all along and its
+  tcp/830 answered SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_8.2 from vManage's own shell in the same
+  measurement. The colour comes from sdwan.color on the link -- two ends of a
+  tunnel must agree on it -- and defaults to "default".
+#}
+vpn 0
+ interface {{ transport_if(loop) }}
+  ip address {{ t.ipv4 }}
+  tunnel-interface
+{% if encap %}
+   encapsulation ipsec
+{% endif %}
+   color {{ t.color | default('default') }}
+   allow-service all
+   allow-service netconf
+   allow-service sshd
+  !
+  no shutdown
+ !
+!
+{% endfor %}
+{% for m in sdwan.mgmt_interfaces | default([]) %}
+{% if loop.index > skip_mgmt %}
+vpn 512
+ interface {{ mgmt_if(loop) }}
+  ip address {{ m.ipv4 }}
+  no shutdown
+ !
+!
+{% endif %}
+{% endfor %}
+{%- endmacro -%}
+{%- macro overlay_routes() -%}
+{#
+  VPN-0 RETURN ROUTES, AS CLI THE INJECTOR COMMITS AFTER LOGIN.
+
+  A controller answers an edge behind a site only if VPN 0 has a route to that
+  edge's transport prefix. The seed cannot carry it (see the note in zcloud),
+  so it rides the same overlay channel the vSmart's transport already uses --
+  the one mechanism proven to apply on these images.
+#}
+{% if sdwan.vpn0_routes is defined and sdwan.vpn0_routes %}
+vpn 0
+{% for r in sdwan.vpn0_routes %}
+ ip route {{ r.prefix }} {{ r.nexthop }}
+{% endfor %}
+!
+{% endif %}
+{%- endmacro -%}
+{%- macro cloudconfig(personality_file, xml, manager, overlay='') -%}
+#cloud-config
+{#
+  DAY-0 GOES DOWN IN bootcmd, AND IS RE-SEEDED IF ConfD BEAT US.
+
+  /etc/confd/init/zcloud.xml carries BOTH the interface configuration and the
+  admin password hash. ConfD reads that directory ONLY when its CDB is empty at
+  first start, guarded by /etc/viptela/cdb_init_done (etc/sv/confd/run). Miss
+  that window and the device has no interface config -- so container-manager's
+  check_interface gate stays shut, the container bundle is never unpacked,
+  dockerd never starts -- AND no valid password, so the console cannot be
+  logged into either.
+
+  MEASURED 2026-09-04. This is a genuine race and BOTH outcomes have been seen:
+    4 vCPU  -- cloud-init finished 08:57:20, cdb_init_done 08:57:25. We won.
+    16 vCPU -- day-0 never applied; console login failed on every attempt.
+  Raising vManage to its documented 16 vCPU minimum made the guest fast enough
+  to lose. The bug was always there; the CPU fix only changed who wins.
+
+  bootcmd ALONE DOES NOT FIX THIS, and it is worth saying why. In this image's
+  /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg, bootcmd and write_files are adjacent entries in
+  cloud_init_modules -- the same `init` stage, which starts AFTER the guest has
+  already printed "Starting services...". Moving the write from write_files to
+  bootcmd buys a few modules, which narrows the window and does not close it.
+
+  So the second half is what actually makes this deterministic: if
+  cdb_init_done already exists when we run, ConfD initialised WITHOUT our seed,
+  and we clear the marker and the CDB and restart it so it re-reads this
+  directory. Whichever way the race went, the device ends up configured.
+
+  Guarded by a once-ever sentinel because bootcmd runs on EVERY boot: without it
+  a later reboot would wipe the CDB of a configured, in-service appliance.
+#}
+bootcmd:
+- |
+  install -d -m 0755 /etc/confd/init
+  cat > /etc/confd/init/zcloud.xml <<'SKYFORGE_ZCLOUD_EOF'
+{{ xml | trim | indent(2, true) }}
+  SKYFORGE_ZCLOUD_EOF
+  chmod 0644 /etc/confd/init/zcloud.xml
+  # Once-ever. bootcmd runs every boot; re-seeding a live appliance would wipe
+  # the running configuration.
+  # NO RESEED. RESTARTING ConfD REBOOTS THE APPLIANCE.
+  #
+  # This block used to clear the CDB and 'sv restart confd' when ConfD had
+  # already initialised without the seed. Measured 2026-09-04 on vBond: sysmgr
+  # treats ConfD dying as fatal and takes the box down --
+  #
+  #   ok: down: /service/confd-p1        ok: down: /service/sysmgr: normally up
+  #   kill: run: /service/confd (pid 754) ... got TERM
+  #   Unmounting all filesystems...      reboot: Restarting system
+  #   Last reboot: Software initiated - ConfD got signal 9.
+  #
+  # -- and the injector correctly reads that reboot as the device refusing its
+  # day-0. The cure was worse than the disease.
+  #
+  # It is also unnecessary. Writing from bootcmd wins the race on its own, by a
+  # margin measured on the same run:
+  #
+  #   14:52:47.021  /etc/confd/init/zcloud.xml
+  #   14:52:54.595  /etc/viptela/cdb_init_done
+  #
+  # 7.5 seconds. bootcmd runs in cloud_init_modules immediately before
+  # write_files, and that is early enough. The reseed was insurance against a
+  # window this evidence says we are not in.
+  #
+  # If a future image DOES lose this race, the fix is NOT to kill ConfD: it is to
+  # deliver day-0 earlier still, or to reboot deliberately once and teach the
+  # injector that one seeded reboot is expected rather than fatal.
+  install -d -m 0755 /etc/viptela
+  : > /etc/viptela/.skyforge-day0-seeded
+{% if manager %}
+disk_setup:
+  /dev/vdb:
+    table_type: mbr
+    layout: false
+    overwrite: false
+fs_setup:
+- device: /dev/vdb
+  label: data
+  partition: none
+  filesystem: ext4
+  overwrite: false
+mounts:
+- [ /dev/vdb, /opt/data ]
+{% endif %}
+write_files:
+{% if manager %}
+- path: /opt/web-app/etc/persona
+  owner: vmanage:vmanage-admin
+  permissions: '0644'
+  content: '{"persona":"COMPUTE_AND_DATA"}'
+{% endif %}
+- path: /etc/default/personality
+  content: "{{ personality_file }}\n"
+- path: /etc/default/inited
+  content: "1\n"
+{% if sdwan.root_ca_pem is defined and sdwan.root_ca_pem %}
+- path: /usr/share/viptela/symantec-root-ca.crt
+  content: ""
+- path: /usr/share/viptela/root-ca.crt
+  content: |
+{{ sdwan.root_ca_pem | trim | indent(4, true) }}
+- path: /home/admin/skyforge-root-ca.pem
+  owner: admin:admin
+  permissions: '0644'
+  content: |
+{{ sdwan.root_ca_pem | trim | indent(4, true) }}
+{% endif %}
+{% if overlay | trim %}
+- path: {{ OVERLAY_PATH }}
+  owner: admin:admin
+  permissions: '0644'
+  content: |
+{{ overlay | trim | indent(4, true) }}
+{% endif %}
+{%- endmacro -%}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.rootca.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.rootca.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6e0c6a2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/initial/cisco_sdwan.rootca.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+{#
+  ENTERPRISE ROOT CA -- the one thing day-0 carries that is not identity.
+
+  WHY THIS IS NOT AN API CALL. Everything else about SD-WAN onboarding belongs
+  to vManage and now runs on the cisco.catalystwan collection. The trust anchor
+  cannot: a full AST enumeration of the SDK found 1025 endpoints across 125
+  files and ZERO matches for chain, trust, anchor, bundle, push or distribute.
+  The only root-CA operations are get/edit_enterprise_root_ca, which store the
+  CA on vManage; the closest device-facing call, install_signed_cert, installs
+  the device's OWN LEAF identity certificate, not an anchor.
+
+  That is measured, not inferred. After doing everything the SDK offers --
+  including change_vedge_list_validity, the step the lane had been missing --
+  `show certificate root-ca-cert | include skyforge` returned NOTHING on both
+  controllers and certificate-status was still Not-Installed. A positive control
+  confirmed the filter worked: `include Subject` returned DigiCert/VeriSign/
+  Viptela SubCA, i.e. the STOCK bundle. `root-ca-chain-status: Installed` refers
+  to that stock bundle and is not evidence that our CA is present.
+
+  WHY IT IS NOT CONFIG EITHER. Installing a trust anchor is an EXEC operation
+  (`request root-cert-chain install <path>`), and there is no configuration
+  stanza for it. Everything above this marker is pasted inside `config` and
+  committed; nothing below it is. The serial injector splits the file here and
+  drives the exec phase itself.
+
+  THE INJECTOR OWNS THE MECHANISM, NOT THIS TEMPLATE. All this emits is a
+  labelled PEM. The injector knows what a `root-ca` payload means and performs
+  the vshell/heredoc/install itself -- deliberately, so that a device template
+  cannot become a channel for arbitrary shell on the appliance.
+
+  PUBLIC HALF ONLY. The private key never appears in a rendered artefact; it
+  stays where signing happens. See sdwan.trust.root_ca_pem.
+
+  Proven on a live vSmart 20.7.1 (2026-09-01): the console places the PEM and
+  `request root-cert-chain install` answers "Successfully installed the root
+  certificate chain", after which `show certificate root-ca-cert | include
+  skyforge` returns our CA. certificate-status stays Not-Installed at that
+  point, correctly -- that field is the device's LEAF, which vManage installs
+  later.
+#}
+{% if sdwan.root_ca_pem is defined and sdwan.root_ca_pem %}
+!! skyforge-post-commit root-ca
+{{ sdwan.root_ca_pem | trim }}
+{% endif %}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/routing/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/routing/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..868f837
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/routing/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+{#
+  Deliberately EMPTY. A Viptela appliance declares routing.static only so the
+  sdwan module can render its VPN-0 default route into DAY-0 (see
+  initial/cisco_sdwan.cloudconfig.j2 and modules/sdwan.py
+  _default_route_nexthop). Nothing is ever pushed to these appliances over
+  Ansible: they sit outside the netlab-initial limit (day-0 arrives on the
