diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/sdwan/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/sdwan/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..21f8bf2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/sdwan/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+{#
+  SD-WAN fabric onboarding INTENT for vManage (Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN Manager).
+
+  This is NOT device configuration and it is never pushed to a device. It is a
+  JSON description of the fabric -- organization, validator address, and the
+  roster of controllers to enroll -- which tasks/cisco_sdwan-manager/sdwan.yml
+  hands to vManage's own REST API. vManage remains the only thing that
+  configures an SD-WAN device; netlab only tells it what the fabric is.
+
+  Everything here is derived by the sdwan module (_push_overlay_facts and
+  _push_onboarding_facts), because a template renders with NODE scope and
+  cannot look up the validator's address or enumerate its peers.
+
+  Rendered as JSON on purpose: the deploy lane consumes it with from_json, so a
+  malformed roster fails at parse time with the file in hand, rather than
+  halfway through a sequence of REST calls that have already changed state.
+
+  PURE JINJA2 ONLY. `netlab create` renders this with its own environment,
+  which has none of ansible's filters -- `to_nice_json` here failed the whole
+  demo-lab compile with "No filter named 'to_nice_json'" (measured 2026-08-31,
+  the same way extra/firewall/policy/panos.j2 failed on ansible's `search`
+  test). `tojson` is Jinja2's own and is the one to use.
+#}
+{{ {
+     "organization": sdwan.onboarding.organization | default(''),
+     "vbond_address": sdwan.onboarding.vbond_address | default(''),
+     "controllers": sdwan.onboarding.controllers | default([]),
+     "edges": sdwan.onboarding.edges | default([]),
+   } | tojson(indent=2) }}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..15a8e87
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+{#
+    cisco_sdwan-controller (Viptela vSmart) renders NOTHING here, deliberately.
+
+    Same reasoning as services/cisco_sdwan_edge.j2. The snmp_config body this
+    replaces was already a documented no-op:
+
+      snmp_config/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2:
+        "SNMP not configured via CLI (uncertain/unsupported vendor SNMP);
+         device is collected over SSH and is SNMP-perf-exempt."
+
+    gnmi_config/cisco_sdwan-controller.j2 is 0 bytes. the collector collects vSmart with the
+    cisco_sdwan_vsmart_ssh collector, so an empty fragment preserves the
+    previous behaviour exactly.
+
+    The file exists so netlab can resolve a services template for every node
+    once groups.all force-adds `module: [ services ]` fleet-wide; without it
+    any topology containing a cisco_sdwan-controller node failed at compile.
+
+    Note vBond is not a separate image or device: it is a vEdge whose day-0
+    selects the role (`system vbond <ip> local vbond-only`), so it resolves
+    through services/cisco_sdwan_edge.j2, not this file.
+#}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2ee237f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/cisco_sdwan-manager.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+{#
+    cisco_sdwan-manager (Viptela vManage) renders NOTHING here, deliberately.
+
+    This file must EXIST even though it is empty. netlab resolves a config
+    template for EVERY node whose module list includes `services`, and
+    groups.all force-adds `module: [ services ]` fleet-wide, so without it any
+    topology containing a cisco_sdwan-manager node dies at compile with "Cannot find
+    services configuration template". Declaring features.services WITHOUT this
+    file moves the failure rather than fixing it -- exactly how patch 0042 first
+    landed for the Versa devices, and how this device first failed:
+
+      IncorrectValue in modules: Device type cisco_sdwan-manager (node vmanage)
+      does not support module services
+
+    It is empty because there is no SNMP agent to configure. the collector does not
+    collect vManage as a device at all -- it has no vManage device type, so
+    vManage is carried as an ENDPOINT (forward_collection: endpoint) and exists
+    in the topology for FABRIC operation: real Viptela needs vManage to
+    provision certificates and admit devices to the overlay.
+#}
